    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Given that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2{x}^{4}-13)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡u,\\,u=2{x}^{4}-13.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\sec^{2}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\sec^{2}⁡u\\cdot 8{x}^{3} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}\\sec^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}\\sec^{2}⁡(2{x}^{4}-13).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/id": "d97305fb-707f-51fd-ad91-a7b62a7a0890",
