    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope $m$ of $y =\\sin \\left(\\pi \\left(8x^{2} + 3x - 2\\right)\\right)$ at $x = 1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +3x - 2\\right).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (16x + 3).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (16x+3)\\\\\n\n&=\\pi (16x+3)\\cos u\\\\\n\n&=\\pi (16x+3)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8x^{2} +3x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (16\\cdot1+3)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(8\\cdot1^{2} +3\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\\n\n& =19\\pi \\cos(9\\pi) \\\\\n&= 19\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= -19\\pi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n$$\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-19\\pi}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "4632c9b8-794b-5d38-a842-0329b9629e46",
